{# The loud half of queue item 51, and of the owner's standing rule: "if there's an issue be clear
   be loud." Moved here from ProcurementBundle 2026-09-17 — a path change and nothing else — now
   that the invoice payments screen wants the same notice for its own `?payment=` edit link that
   the bill payments screen already had.

   Rendered by every screen that takes an id in its URL and decides what a form is about from it:
   the purchase side's seven create screens and its two row links that put a form into EDIT mode
   (`/bills/{id}/payments?payment=`, `/vendors/{id}?address=`), and now the sell side's own
   `/invoice/{id}/payments?payment=`. Those fall back to a CREATE (or blank) form when the id
   resolves to nothing, which is the same silence with somebody's money or master data behind it.

   `parents` is the list of RequestedParent objects that were REQUESTED and resolved to nothing —
   normally empty, in which case this partial draws nothing at all.

   ## Three things about where and how this is drawn

   1. **Not a flash.** `CompanyListScope` makes the argument on the sell side and it holds here: a
      flash is a toast a reader can miss, and the form underneath it looks exactly like a form that
      is working. This sits in the page, above the fields it is about, and stays there on reload.

   2. **Not a `.lead`, and not inside `.panel-title-row`.** When this was written core's admin skin
      hid both there — `.site-admin .panel-title-row .lead { display: none !important }` — so every
      procurement screen's lead sentence was invisible and this notice would have been too. Item 41
      has since landed and removed that rule. The placement stays anyway: an error is not a subtitle
      for the page heading, it belongs above the form it is about, and the title row is now where
      STATUS lives.

   3. **`alert error` and nothing else.** Colour is a shared, core-owned trait vocabulary (owner
      ruling, item 42): a screen does not invent a class, and "this is broken" already has one. #}
{% if parents is not empty %}
    <section class="panel" id="bad-parent-notice">
        {% for parent in parents %}
            <p class="alert error">{{ parent.notice }}</p>
        {% endfor %}
    </section>
{% endif %}
